    environment: "underground",
    visualDescription: "This creature is a stocky humanoid standing about 5 or 6 feet tall with sooty-black hair and green eyes. It wears crudely constructed hides of fur and leather. Its face has a curious, almost child-like appearance and seems to be imprinted with a permanent, non-changing smile. Its skin is pinkish-white.",
    description: "Lava children make their lairs deep underground and usually in warmer climates. Some lava children build their communities in dying or burned out volcanoes as well. Their society as a whole is reclusive, and rarely do lava children have dealings with outside races (magmin and fire elementals being the exception). Lava children attack with their clawed hands and vicious bite. They direct their attacks against the most heavily armored foe (as their attacks can pass through armor) in an attempt to weaken their opponent's strongest (and probably front line) combatants. Lava Children as Characters Lava Children possess racial Hit Dice and may have class levels. Lava Children have the following racial traits. +2 Strength, +2 Constitution. Lava children are strong and hardy. Darkvision: Lava children can see in the dark up to 60 feet. Racial Hit Dice: A lava child begins with four levels of humanoid, which provide 4d8 Hit Dice, a base attack bonus of +3, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +4, Ref +1, and Will +1. Racial Skills: A lava child's humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 4 x (2 + Int modifier). Racial Feats: A lava child's humanoid levels give it two feats. AC: +6 natural armor bonus. Natural Weapons: 2 claws (1d4) and bite (1d6). Special Attacks: Rend (2d4+1) (see above). Defensive Abilities: Elemental vulnerability, immunity to earth magic, immunity to fire, immunity to metal, elemental traits, vulnerability to cold (see above). Languages: Ignan, Lava Child. Lava children with high Intelligence scores can choose from the following languages: Dwarf, Goblin, Orc, Terran. Credit The Lava Children originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission. Copyright Notice Author Scott Greene, based on original material by Jim Donohoe.",
